<p>While <a href="/zelda-minish-cap/">Zelda: Minish Cap</a> didn’t receive the same mainstream hype as <em>Ocarina of Time</em>, its clever size‑shifting mechanic makes it a standout on the GBA. The Reddit user <strong>zelda_lover</strong> rightly points out that it’s still underrated, and playing it in a browser can bring that hidden gem to a wider audience.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>What makes it perfect for the web</strong>: The game’s puzzles are puzzle‑heavy rather than reflex‑heavy, meaning you can pause at any time without breaking immersion.</li>
  <li><strong>Browser‑specific advice</strong>:
    <ol>
      <li>Enable the emulator’s <em>save state</em> function before entering a new dungeon. The Minish Cap’s dungeons are labyrinthine, and a quick reload can save you minutes.</li>
      <li>If you’re on a mobile device, use the on‑screen D‑pad for precise platforming, but consider connecting a Bluetooth controller for smoother movement.</li>
    </ol>
  </li>
</ul>
<p>Fans of classic Zelda titles will also enjoy <a href="/zelda-a-link-to-the-past/">A Link to the Past</a>, which is available on EggerMath and offers a nostalgic bridge between the SNES and GBA eras.</p>

<h2>3. Metroid Fusion – A Sci‑Fi Thriller That Still Holds Up</h2>
<p>Metroid Fusion is the game that <strong>metroid_fan</strong> remembers as a childhood nightmare—thanks to its atmospheric storytelling and tight platforming. Even after 20 years, the game feels fresh because of its seamless blend of exploration and action.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Why Fusion works great in a browser</strong>: The game’s relatively short levels load instantly, and the emulator’s audio sync is spot‑on, preserving the eerie soundtrack that defines the Metroid vibe.</li>
  <li><strong>Pro tips for online play</strong>:
